<b>These don't.</b>

234
00:08:25,733 --> 00:08:27,066
<b>That isn't evidence.</b>

235
00:08:27,199 --> 00:08:28,766
<b>It's shopping for it.</b>

236
00:08:29,399 --> 00:08:31,466
<b>This shape was never the real story.</b>

237
00:08:31,666 --> 00:08:33,500
<b>This map sat forgotten in a palace</b>

238
00:08:33,500 --> 00:08:35,666
<b>library for 400 years.</b>

239
00:08:35,966 --> 00:08:36,766
<b>Not hidden.</b>

240
00:08:37,066 --> 00:08:38,133
<b>Not suppressed.</b>

241
00:08:38,500 --> 00:08:39,866
<b>Just ignored.</b>

242
00:08:40,500 --> 00:08:41,600
<b>And being ignored is the</b>

243
00:08:41,600 --> 00:08:43,000
<b>only thing that saved it.</b>

244
00:08:43,300 --> 00:08:45,533
<b>Piri Reis was drawing this map for an</b>

245
00:08:45,533 --> 00:08:46,933
<b>empire whose government,</b>

246
00:08:47,300 --> 00:08:50,500
<b>the Porte, the Sultan, and his court had</b>

247
00:08:50,500 --> 00:08:51,933
<b>no use for what he did.</b>

248
00:08:52,399 --> 00:08:54,800
<b>In the same century, Spain and Portugal</b>

249
00:08:54,866 --> 00:08:56,466
<b>each ran a government office</b>

250
00:08:56,466 --> 00:08:58,866
<b>where each voyage's findings got added to</b>

251
00:08:58,866 --> 00:09:00,199
<b>a single master chart,</b>

252
00:09:00,533 --> 00:09:02,800
<b>kept up to date by paid cartographers.</b>

253
00:09:03,433 --> 00:09:04,800
<b>The Ottoman state had nothing</b>

254
00:09:04,800 --> 00:09:07,399
<b>like that, and didn't want it.</b>

255
00:09:07,899 --> 00:09:09,566
<b>As Svat Soucek puts it,</b>

256
00:09:09,966 --> 00:09:12,633
<b>"Piri Reis's maps represented a world</b>

257
00:09:12,666 --> 00:09:13,633
<b>that lay beyond the</b>

258
00:09:13,633 --> 00:09:15,300
<b>horizon of the Porte's ambition.</b>

259
00:09:16,199 --> 00:09:18,366
<b>The map was rolled up and shelved."</b>

260
00:09:19,033 --> 00:09:22,699
<b>As for Piri Reis, around 1553, in his</b>

261
00:09:22,699 --> 00:09:24,933
<b>80s, he lost a sea battle.</b>

262
00:09:25,166 --> 00:09:26,399
<b>The governor of Egypt</b>

263
00:09:26,433 --> 00:09:27,933
<b>reported him to the Sultan.</b>

264
00:09:28,233 --> 00:09:30,566
<b>He died never knowing that the map he'd</b>

265
00:09:30,600 --> 00:09:31,899
<b>drawn was still</b>

266
00:09:31,899 --> 00:09:33,433
<b>sitting where he'd left it.</b>

267
00:09:34,266 --> 00:09:35,333
<b>Soucek calls it,</b>

268
00:09:35,733 --> 00:09:38,433
<b>"a strange map whose mutilated remains</b>

269
00:09:38,433 --> 00:09:39,300
<b>lay forgotten in the</b>

270
00:09:39,300 --> 00:09:41,166
<b>library of Topkapı Palace."</b>

271
00:09:41,966 --> 00:09:43,866
<b>And there it stayed, in a storeroom</b>

272
00:09:43,866 --> 00:09:44,733
<b>nobody had properly</b>

273
00:09:44,733 --> 00:09:46,500
<b>catalogued until 1929,</b>

274
00:09:47,033 --> 00:09:48,833
<b>when the palace was being turned into a</b>

275
00:09:48,833 --> 00:09:51,033
<b>museum and the director found it.</b>

276
00:09:51,666 --> 00:09:55,333
<b>It reached Paul Kahle, who, by luck, had</b>

277
00:09:55,333 --> 00:09:56,500
<b>spent the previous years</b>

278
00:09:56,500 --> 00:09:57,966
<b>on Piri Reis's other book,</b>

279
00:09:58,233 --> 00:09:59,300
<b>the Sailing Manual.</b>

280
00:09:59,733 --> 00:10:01,699
<b>He knew the hand the moment he saw it,</b>

281
00:10:01,933 --> 00:10:03,266
<b>and he caught what no</b>

282
00:10:03,266 --> 00:10:05,033
<b>one had in four centuries.</b>

283
00:10:05,600 --> 00:10:08,100
<b>The map says, in writing, that it was</b>

284
00:10:08,100 --> 00:10:09,399
<b>partially copied from</b>

285
00:10:09,399 --> 00:10:10,766
<b>a chart by Columbus.</b>

286
00:10:11,466 --> 00:10:12,633
<b>So what does the Piri</b>

287
00:10:12,633 --> 00:10:14,233
<b>Reis map actually say?</b>

288
00:10:14,699 --> 00:10:16,866
<b>It tells you where every line on it came</b>

289
00:10:16,866 --> 00:10:18,399
<b>from, and what every</b>

290
00:10:18,399 --> 00:10:20,033
<b>piece of land really is.</b>

291
00:10:20,666 --> 00:10:22,100
<b>Piri Reis wrote it down</b>

292
00:10:22,100 --> 00:10:25,300
<b>himself in 1513 on the parchment.</b>

293
00:10:25,733 --> 00:10:27,066
<b>We just didn't read it.</b>

